IMPLEMENTED FROM PUBLISHED DESCRIPTIONS
=======================================

MotionMapper
  Method:      the behavioral mapping of Berman et al.: a wavelet
               spectrogram of posture, embedded in two dimensions with
               t-SNE, then segmented into behavioral states. Mosaic uses
               no MotionMapper code; the steps are written from the
               published description.
  In:          src/mosaic/behavior/feature_library/, the feature chain
               pair-posedistance-pca -> pair-wavelet -> extract-templates
               -> global-scaler -> global-tsne -> global-kmeans |
               global-ward.
  Differences: four.
               1. The postural basis is rotation-invariant Euclidean
               distances between keypoints, within each animal of a pair
               and across the two, reduced by incremental PCA. It is not
               the eigenmodes of single-animal images.
               2. pair-wavelet takes a complex Morlet spectrogram over a
               default band of 0.2 to 5 Hz.
               3. No step normalizes each time point's spectrogram.
               pair-wavelet writes the log of the wavelet power, floored,
               and global-scaler standardizes each column across the
               templates.
               4. Behavioral states come from k-means or Ward clustering
               of the scaled templates, not from a watershed segmentation
               of a density estimate of the embedding.
  Citation:    Berman, G. J., Choi, D. M., Bialek, W., Shaevitz, J. W.
               (2014). Mapping the stereotyped behaviour of freely moving
               fruit flies. Journal of the Royal Society Interface
               11:20140672. https://doi.org/10.1098/rsif.2014.0672


