The remaining runtime and development dependencies are permissively
licensed (BSD, MIT, Apache-2.0, PSF), with the exceptions below that a
default install does reach.

certifi is MPL-2.0, and arrives unconditionally on every platform:
mosaic-behavior -> tables -> blosc2 -> httpx -> certifi. MPL-2.0 is
file-level copyleft. It reaches modifications to certifi's own files,
which mosaic does not make, and not the work that depends on it, so it
imposes nothing here beyond keeping the notice.

NVIDIA components reach a default install through xgboost. On Linux,
xgboost declares nvidia-nccl-cu12 for GPU support. The license file its
wheel ships is BSD-3-Clause, although the metadata of the newest releases
declares NVIDIA proprietary terms. xgboost's own wheels for Linux and
Windows also contain the NVIDIA CUDA runtime, statically linked, which is
proprietary and redistributable under the CUDA Toolkit End User License
Agreement (https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/eula/). mosaic never runs xgboost
on the GPU.

The NVIDIA CUDA libraries that arrive with PyTorch on Linux, under the
deep-learning, feral and lightning-action extras, are proprietary under
the same EULA; cuDNN and cuSPARSELt fall under NVIDIA's SDK license
agreement instead, and NVTX is Apache-2.0. NVIDIA DALI and nvImageCodec,
under the lightning-action extra, also carry proprietary NVIDIA
components despite their Apache-2.0 labels. mosaic reaches CUDA only
through PyTorch and redistributes none of it.

The resolved set is recorded in uv.lock, and

    python scripts/gen_third_party_inventory.py

regenerates the full inventory from it: per extra and per dependency
group, the transitive closure with each distribution's license resolved
from PyPI metadata and flagged when it is not permissive. It reads license
metadata only, so it does not see libraries bundled inside a wheel -- the
FFmpeg builds in av and opencv-python, or the CUDA runtime in xgboost --
which are described above instead. By default it reads mosaic's lock,
the isolated keypoint-MoSeq lock and the isolated VAME lock. Pass --lock
for the Ultralytics and POLO environment locks, which list the
AGPL-licensed distributions and are not read by default.
